<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and Discriminative
<br class="ltx_break">Cancer Survival Prediction<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>This work was supported by the National Undergraduate Training Program for Innovation and Entrepreneurship under Grant 202510336053, the Fundamental Research Funds for
the Provincial Universities of Zhejiang under Grant GK259909299001-006, and the Anhui Provincial Joint Construction Key Laboratory of Intelligent Education Equipment and Technology under Grant IEET202401. <span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Corresponding authors: Zhu Zhu, Yifei Chen, and Feiwei Qin)</span></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Shuo Jiang, Zhuwen Chen, Liaoman Xu, and Feiwei Qin are with Hangzhou Dianzi University, Hangzhou 310018, China (e-mail: {jiangshuo; 24050924; 23320302; qinfeiwei}@hdu.edu.cn).</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Yanming Zhu is with the School of Information and Communication Technology, Griffith University, QLD 4215, Australia (e-mail: yanming.zhu@griffith.edu.au).</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Changmiao Wang is with Shenzhen Research Institute of Big Data, Shenzhen 518172, China (e-mail: cmwangalbert@gmail.com).</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Jiong Zhang is with the Laboratory of Advanced Theranostic Materials and Technology, Ningbo Institute of Materials Technology and Engineering, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Ningbo 315201, China (e-mail: jiong.zhang@ieee.org).</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Yifei Chen is with the School of Biomedical Engineering, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China (e-mail: justlfc03@gmail.com).</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Zhu Zhu is with the National Clinical Research Center for Child Health, National Children’s Regional Medical Center, Children’s Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou 310052, Zhejiang, China (e-mail: zhuzhu_cs@zju.edu.cn).</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shuo Jiang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhuwen Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Liaoman Xu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yanming Zhu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Changmiao Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Jiong Zhang, Feiwei Qin, Yifei Chen, and Zhu Zhu







</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Survival analysis plays a vital role in making clinical decisions. However, the models currently in use are often difficult to interpret, which reduces their usefulness in clinical settings. Prototype learning presents a potential solution, yet traditional methods focus on local similarities and static matching, neglecting the broader tumor context and lacking strong semantic alignment with genomic data. To overcome these issues, we introduce an innovative prototype-based multimodal framework, FeatProto, aimed at enhancing cancer survival prediction by addressing significant limitations in current prototype learning methodologies within pathology. Our framework establishes a unified feature prototype space that integrates both global and local features of whole slide images (WSI) with genomic profiles. This integration facilitates traceable and interpretable decision-making processes. Our approach includes three main innovations: (1) A robust phenotype representation that merges critical patches with global context, harmonized with genomic data to minimize local bias. (2) An Exponential Prototype Update Strategy (EMA ProtoUp) that sustains stable cross-modal associations and employs a wandering mechanism to adapt prototypes flexibly to tumor heterogeneity. (3) A hierarchical prototype matching scheme designed to capture global centrality, local typicality, and cohort-level trends, thereby refining prototype inference. Comprehensive evaluations on four publicly available cancer datasets indicate that our method surpasses current leading unimodal and multimodal survival prediction techniques in both accuracy and interoperability, providing a new perspective on prototype learning for critical medical applications. Our source code is available at <a href="https://github.com/JSLiam94/FeatProto" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/JSLiam94/FeatProto</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Cancer Survival Prediction, Prototype Learning, Multimodal Learning, Interpretability

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/compare_p.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Schematic of survival prediction. (a) Multimodal feature embedding &amp; fusion; (b) Conventional survival prediction (decision head: linear fully-connected layer); (c) Proposed feature prototype learning: prototype library with global-local fused features and multilevel deep prototype matching for accurate, interpretable survival prediction; (d) Traditional prototype library (local image only).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Survival analysis plays a crucial role in cancer prognosis modeling, with its primary goal of assessing the probability of endpoint events (e.g. death) in specific patients over a future period and, accordingly, accurately stratifying patients’ risk levels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This analysis not only reveals potential associations between disease progression and treatment responses, but also provides solid data support for formulating personalized treatment strategies that hold significant scientific research and clinical value <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. In recent years, the rapid development of deep learning has driven the expansion of survival analysis from traditional clinical variables to a multimodal data paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. In particular, models that integrate pathological images from whole slide imaging (WSI) and genomic characteristics have become an essential direction for improving the precision of prognostic prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The former provides visual clues regarding cellular morphology, tissue structure, and tumor heterogeneity, while the latter reveals biological mechanisms at the molecular level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. The two exhibit inherent and profound complementarity, facilitating synergistic and holistic modeling of cancer progression from both phenotypic traits and genotypic profiles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the initial performance achievements of existing multimodal survival analysis methods, most models still employ typical “black-box” deep architectures, which lack sufficient interpretability, thereby limiting their application in clinical practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. To address this issue, recent studies have emerged to improve model interpretability, including post-hoc explanation methods and intrinsically interpretable prototype learning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. The former performs post-hoc feature analysis on prediction results, while the latter introduces prototype learning during model training, aiming to bridge the gap between the model’s latent feature space and human-understandable semantics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. However, traditional prototype learning methods typically rely on similarity analysis of local image regions, locating key regions through attention mechanisms, and performing visual matching with predefined prototype libraries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Such methods have particularly prominent limitations in the field of cancer pathology. Global features of the tumor microenvironment in WSI images are essential for prognosis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, but relying on single local information can introduce biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. Additionally, the static nature and poor semantic alignment of prototype libraries can hinder the model’s ability to identify key regions, impacting its discriminative power and interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, this paper introduces an innovative approach to cancer survival analysis through a method that utilizes feature prototype learning, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The contributions of this study are threefold.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a unified prototype space that effectively encodes both local and global weighted features from WSI and genomic profile representations. This integration facilitates precise and interpretable prognostic modeling, offering traceable pathways for decision-making and enhancing phenotypic characterization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an Exponential Moving Average-based Prototype Update strategy (EMA ProtoUp). This strategy ensures the stability and adaptability of cross-modal associations. Additionally, our mechanism for Wandering Prototypes allows these prototypes to dynamically explore semantically relevant areas, which effectively addresses the challenge of tumor heterogeneity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a multilevel deep prototype matching strategy. This strategy assesses cross-modal similarity from various perspectives, thereby enhancing prototype discrimination, expanding the diversity of the prototype library, and increasing the adaptability of the model.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Application of Multimodal Data Fusion in Cancer Survival Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a critical component of precision medicine, cancer survival analysis has made remarkable progress in recent years, driven by the rapid advancement of deep learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. In particular, fusion of data from various modalities, such as WSI of pathological tissues and genomic data, enabled a more comprehensive characterization of tumor heterogeneity, thereby significantly improving prediction accuracy and clinical applicability. Genomic data, which provided molecular-level insights into cancer initiation and progression, have been widely used in tumor risk assessment and prognostic prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. On the other hand, pathological images, rich in visual information, have become a pivotal modality in cancer diagnosis and survival analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. To fully exploit these complementary data sources, researchers have proposed various cross-modal fusion strategies. For instance, MOTCat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> employed optimal transport theory to realize global alignment of cross-modal features. Cross-modal translation-alignment framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> captured intra-modal information via parallel encoding-decoding structures and used cross-modal attention as a bridge to transfer complementary information. A memory-efficient multimodal Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> was used to fuse pathway and histological patch information, modeling inter-modal interactions for survival prediction. Multimodal knowledge decomposition (MKD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> effectively separated redundant and modality-specific components, using cohort-guided modeling (CGM) to improve generalization performance by reducing noise. However, many multimodal models remain “black boxes”, which complicates their use in clinical decision-making. Thus, how to effectively fuse multimodal information while offering an intuitive explanatory mechanism remains a critical and pressing challenge in multimodal survival analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/update_design.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:952/443;" width="952" height="443" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prototype Library Construction and Update. Left: Prototype library construction. The initial model generates feature embeddings to derive a similarity matrix, which is clustered to form a central prototype library. Feature prototypes are categorized by survival risk levels. Middle: EMA ProtoUp. Old prototypes migrate toward new features via EMA-based updates to generate new prototypes, representing typical samples. Right: Design and update mechanism of Wandering Prototypes. During prototype library construction or update, edge cases are selected from new features to represent special samples.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Research Progress in Explainable Methods and Prototype Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the prevalent “black-box” issue in deep learning models, explainable methods have garnered extensive attention. These methods are generally categorized into two types: post-hoc explanation and intrinsic explainability. Post-hoc explanation methods, such as Grad-CAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and SHAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, analyzed the decisions of trained models and visualized regions of model focus through heatmaps. Intrinsic explainability methods enhance a model’s interpretability by incorporating understandable structures during training, like prototype learning. This classic approach builds decision-making on “explaining with examples.” For instance, Prototypical Networks for few-shot learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> performed classification by calculating the similarity between input samples and prototypes. ProtoPNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> utilizes several typical prototype images. The model classifies images based on similarity comparisons with prototype images, enabling interpretable decision-making. Feature prototypes were introduced to represent discriminative features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, letting models characterize their attributes across individuals and explain key image features for classification. While ProtoArgNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and MGProto <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> have advanced prototype learning, current methods primarily focus on classification through local similarity matching, limiting their effectiveness in survival analysis, which requires both global and local information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exploration and Limitations of Prototype Learning in Medical Image Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prototype learning holds enormous potential in the medical field, and preliminary studies have demonstrated that its explicit interpretive pathways can effectively boost clinical acceptance. For instance, PIP-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> enhanced the reliability and acceptability of the model by interpreting and correcting medical image classification results, while Proto-BagNets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> enabled transparent decision-making through joint local-global feature analysis. In the critical task of cancer survival prediction, prototype-based multimodal approaches have further advanced this direction: the MultiModal Prototyping (MMP) framework leverages morphological prototypes from WSIs and biological pathway prototypes from transcriptomic data for efficient multimodal fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>; The Prototypical Information Bottlenecking and Disentangling (PIBD) framework applies prototypical learning to select discriminative intra-modal instances and disentangle modality-common or specific knowledge, further optimizing multimodal cancer survival prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>. Despite these progresses, current mainstream cancer analysis methods still primarily concentrate on single imaging modalities and classification tasks, overlooking survival analysis that integrates imaging and genomic data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. Traditional approaches often overlook global structural information and struggle to address cancer heterogeneity, thereby limiting their predictive performance. There remains an urgent need for a robust multimodal framework that seamlessly integrates both global and local features derived from WSIs and multi-omics genomic data: this integration not only enhances prediction accuracy and interpretability, but also resolves complex associations related to tumor progression and patient prognosis.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overvall Architecture</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-A Application of Multimodal Data Fusion in Cancer Survival Analysis ‣ II Related Work ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the overall architecture of our proposed Feature Prototype Library (FeatProto), which is designed to model category-specific representative features explicitly. We employed the state-of-the-art (SOTA) survival prediction model and removed the fully connected linear head used for decision-making as the backbone for the feature extraction and fusion module of WSI and genome. The extracted features were then used to create and update the prototype library in the subsequent steps. The Feature Prototype Library stores <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{proto}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{proto}</annotation></semantics></math> representative features per survival analysis category as prototypes, each from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{lib}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{lib}</annotation></semantics></math> WSI Bags and genetic info fusion/evolution. For survival prediction, multi-strategy deep comparison integrates max prototype similarity, category average similarity, and center similarity to better determine risk levels, boosting the model’s discriminative power. Tracing decision-related prototypes and their evolution yields representative samples for auxiliary diagnosis. Unlike traditional models’ linear layers, this architecture remarkably enhances decision interpretability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This feature prototype library includes both typical and Wandering Prototypes to represent the central tendency of features and intra-class diversity. It allows dynamic updates to be adapted to evolving models. The following sections will discuss its design, initialization, update mechanism, and integration with survival models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Design of the Feature Prototype Library</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Feature Prototype serves as a ”feature anchor” representing a specific survival risk category in the multimodal feature space. It is defined explicitly as a set of feature vectors that can characterize the core attributes (typical prototypes) or marginal heterogeneity (Wandering Prototypes) of survival risk categories within a unified space that integrates global-local features of WSI and genomic features. Its core function is to establish traceable decision-making paths through ”input sample-prototype similarity matching,” thereby associating model predictions with materialized features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The feature prototype library is crafted to house prototypes specific to each category. It includes typical prototypes, which capture the essential representative features of categories, and Wandering Prototypes, which capture diverse and atypical features within classes. This dual-prototype approach balances representativeness with diversity, thereby improving the model’s ability to generalize to unseen data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Typical prototype.</span> For each category, represent the most central features, encoding essential characteristics of the category as “prototype samples” for classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wandering Prototype.</span> Selected from regions moderately distant from the category center (within predefined boundaries), not overlapping with other regions; they capture in-class exceptional cases, compensate for over-reliance on central features, and enhance edge-case robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The feature prototype library can be formalized as a tuple <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{lib}=(P,W,I,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{lib}=(P,W,I,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times K_{proto}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times K_{proto}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes typical prototypes (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>: number of categories; <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{proto}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{proto}</annotation></semantics></math>: number of prototypes per category; <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>: feature dimension);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times M{wander}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times M{wander}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes Wandering Prototypes (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{wander}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{wander}</annotation></semantics></math>: number of Wandering Prototypes per category);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapping from prototype IDs to original data (including type and category index);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> stores auxiliary information, such as mapping of associated data paths, for traceable interpretability analysis.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Initialization of the Prototype Library</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prototypes are initialized from training data in a data-driven manner. For each class <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the extraction and fusion modules of the survival analysis model, WSI and genomic information are integrated to generate features belonging to class <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}=x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid y_{i}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}=x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid y_{i}=c</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label of sample <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The class center is calculated as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}=\frac{1}{|X_{c}|}\sum_{x\in X_{c}}x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}=\frac{1}{|X_{c}|}\sum_{x\in X_{c}}x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the Power Mean Distance Similarity(PMDSim), where we first increase each distance <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th power, thus significantly amplifying the differences between features and emphasizing the importance of distance for prototypes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p}(a,b)=\frac{1}{1+\left(\frac{1}{D}\sum_{i=1}^{D}||a_{i}-b_{i}||^{m}\right)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p}(a,b)=\frac{1}{1+\left(\frac{1}{D}\sum_{i=1}^{D}||a_{i}-b_{i}||^{m}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the feature dimension, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="||\cdot||" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\cdot||</annotation></semantics></math> represents the element-level absolute difference, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> means prototype.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Power operation enhances the weights of key features in the similarity calculation, better reflecting pathologically essential differences. Post-power mean calculation reduces noise/irrelevant feature interference. After L2 normalization of features <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, calculate the PMDSim with <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and select the top <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> features with the highest similarity for class <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For Wandering Prototypes, exclude features already chosen as typical and compute the average distance <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> to the center <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define an ideal distance range <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\bar{d}_{c}-\epsilon,\bar{d}_{c}+\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\bar{d}_{c}-\epsilon,\bar{d}_{c}+\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> to balance intraclass distance and edge particularity, ensuring they don’t overlap with other classes. Select the top <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{wander}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{wander}</annotation></semantics></math> features within this range as Wandering Prototypes, highlighting edge features and exceptional cases. Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 10% of <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">EMA Prototype Library Update Strategy</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An update of the prototype library is performed every <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> epochs. The basic update method is as follows: For each of the top <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> representative features <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in class <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> generated in the current epoch, compute the average dissimilarity <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with all feature prototypes <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the existing library and the dissimilarity <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> with the class center, respectively. If <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the farthest prototype <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{replace}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{replace}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced with a prototype as determined by <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, discarding the least representative prototype.</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=1/S_{p}(f,p_{i}),d_{c}=1/S_{p}(f,\mu_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=1/S_{p}(f,p_{i}),d_{c}=1/S_{p}(f,\mu_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{replace}}=\arg\max_{i}(d_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>replace</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{replace}}=\arg\max_{i}(d_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Model updates cause prototype library lag, as the model’s features change, but some prototypes remain from pre-update models. Basic updates reconstruct the same-size libraries with post-update features, fix lag, but bring abrupt spatial changes, harming training stability and generalization. Incremental updates retain unrepresentative old prototypes, hurting predictions and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the EMA ProtoUp to address the aforementioned issues. This strategy uses a decay factor <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,0.5)</annotation></semantics></math> to lessen the impact of historical prototypes when new features are added. Rather than simple replacements, new features apply EMA to the most similar typical prototypes in the library. Additionally, Wandering Prototypes are selected from the latest features during each update, ensuring the uniqueness of edge cases. The Prototype Source Library stores the ID and path of the source sample for each feature prototype.</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{new}}=\lambda\cdot P_{\mathrm{old}}+(1-\lambda)\cdot f_{\mathrm{new}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>new</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>new</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{new}}=\lambda\cdot P_{\mathrm{old}}+(1-\lambda)\cdot f_{\mathrm{new}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The design of the prototype library is structured to allow for gradual evolution without abrupt changes. It balances past experiences with current data, ensuring stability while still being responsive to new features. Moreover, it tracks the top <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> feature sources and ranks their contributions, which helps clarify the evolution of the prototype while enhancing transparency and interpretability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multilevel Deep Prototype Matching Strategy</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional prototype learning relies solely on the simplistic matching strategy of a single nearest prototype, which is highly vulnerable to adverse effects from outlier samples, resulting in notably low survival prediction accuracy. Meanwhile, relying solely on the matching strategy of the nearest class center has notable significant drawbacks when inter-class feature clustering is unclear in the early stages of training, which can easily lead to severe model underfitting. To address these issues, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-E Multilevel Deep Prototype Matching Strategy ‣ III Method ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we propose the Multilevel deep Prototype Matching strategy (MPMatch). Through the fusion of similarities of features at different levels of the prototype library, optimal survival prediction decisions are derived.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/match.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/341;" width="476" height="341" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Demonstration of the multilevel deep prototype matching strategy. Through weighted fusion of center similarity, class average similarity, and nearest prototype similarity, it synergistically optimizes local feature alignment and global class guidance. Meanwhile, through similarity visualization, it further enhances the interpretability of the model.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve deep matching between features and the prototype library, robust decision-making relies on multilevel similarity fusion and improved interpretability. First, device adaptation and dimension normalization are performed on the input feature tensor. For each class, an effective feature prototype set is dynamically constructed by fusing typical prototypes <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and Wandering Prototypes <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in the effective feature prototype set <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{lib,c}=P_{c}\cup W_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{lib,c}=P_{c}\cup W_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a total of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> prototypes per class.
For the input feature <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature prototype set of class <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the PMDSim, the matching degree between the input feature and each feature prototype <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c,i}</annotation></semantics></math> is measured and converted into a similarity matrix:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{b,c}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times T_{c}},S_{p}(f_{b},p_{c,i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{b,c}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times T_{c}},S_{p}(f_{b},p_{c,i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To balance local prototype matching and global class guidance, we design a three-level similarity fusion strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class average similarity</span> calculates the mean of the feature prototype set, which reflects the overall matching compatibility between the feature and class prototypes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{S}_{b,c}=\frac{1}{T_{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{T_{c}}S_{p}(f_{b},p_{c,i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{S}_{b,c}=\frac{1}{T_{c}}\sum_{i=1}^{T_{c}}S_{p}(f_{b},p_{c,i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nearest prototype similarity</span> reflects local key responses, extracts the maximum similarity from the prototype set, focuses on the most prominently matched prototype, and achieves local feature alignment.</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{max},b,c}=\max_{i=1}^{T_{c}}S_{p}(f_{b},p_{c,i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munderover><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{max},b,c}=\max_{i=1}^{T_{c}}S_{p}(f_{b},p_{c,i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class center similarity</span> introduces global constraints through the mean center of intra-class feature prototypes, guides the overall direction of decision-making, and avoids local prototype bias.</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{center},b,c}=S_{p}(f_{b},\mu_{c})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>center</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{center},b,c}=S_{p}(f_{b},\mu_{c}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By weighted fusion of the above three distinct levels of similarities using predefined weights <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the final survival prediction decision for the sample <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> in class <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained as <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Logit_{b,c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Logit_{b,c}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Logit_{b,c}=\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot\bar{S}_{b,c}+\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{max},b,c}+\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{center},b,c}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>center</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Logit_{b,c}=\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot\bar{S}_{b,c}+\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{max},b,c}+\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}\cdot S_{\mathrm{center},b,c}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III-E Multilevel Deep Prototype Matching Strategy ‣ III Method ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this strategy not only balances local feature alignment and global class guidance to enhance the robustness of decision-making but also provides a basis for decision-making through meta-information, constructing an interpretable closed loop of “feature matching - prototype tracing - clinical interpretation”. It provides strong support for prototype library iteration and decision interpretability in survival analysis scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The risk score <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, which quantifies the likelihood of an event occurrence over time, is computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h(s)=-\sum_{t}\left(\prod_{i=1}^{t}\left(1-\sigma(Logit_{i})\right)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(s)=-\sum_{t}\left(\prod_{i=1}^{t}\left(1-\sigma(Logit_{i})\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid activation function, transforming the model-generated logits (<math id="S3.SS5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Logits_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Logits_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) into hazard rates for each time point <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{t}(1-\sigma(Logit_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{t}(1-\sigma(Logit_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> represents the survival function at time <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing the cumulative probability of event non-occurrence up to time <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> through the product of (1 - hazard rate) values across all preceding time points. The overall risk score is derived by summing these survival probabilities over all time points and taking the negative of the result, such that higher values correspond to an increased risk of event occurrence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Multilevel Deep Feature Prototype Library Matching with Explanation</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Query Features <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, Prototype Library <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Logits and Explanation Dictionary

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Initialize logits as zero tensor [batch_size, num_classes];

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             Initialize prototype origins storage (each one retains <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> source prototypes).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each query feature <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each class index_idx <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               Compute distances (L2) and similarities between <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and all prototypes.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               Calculate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{center}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>center</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{center}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="final\_sim=\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}\times\bar{S}+\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}\times S_{\mathrm{max}}+\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}\times S_{\mathrm{center}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>sim</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>center</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">final\_sim=\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}\times\bar{S}+\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}\times S_{\mathrm{max}}+\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}\times S_{\mathrm{center}}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               Update logits[<math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="batch\_idx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">batch\_idx</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="class\_idx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">class\_idx</annotation></semantics></math>].

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               Tag nearest prototype type (“typical”/“wandering”) and record its ID.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               Track origins: retrieve L source prototypes for the nearest prototype, including contribution weights.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  logits, similarity matrix, IDs-types, and source prototypes with contribution weights for explainability.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">III-F</span> </span><span id="S3.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prototype-Survival Fusion Loss</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We developed the Prototype-Survival fusion Loss (ProtoSurv Loss) for survival prediction, combining prototype learning and survival analysis, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-F Prototype-Survival Fusion Loss ‣ III Method ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prototype Contrastive Loss boosts feature similarity within the same class and increases dissimilarity between different classes, promoting compactness and separability in the feature space. For each sample feature <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, all effective prototypes of the same class (including typical and Wandering Prototypes) are defined as positive prototypes <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and all effective prototypes of other classes are defined as negative prototypes <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. Through contrastive learning, ProtoSurv Loss promotes maximizing the similarity between <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ij}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ij}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature, and minimizing the similarity <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ij}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ij}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> with negative prototypes.</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ij}^{+}=S_{p}(f_{i},p_{j}^{+}),S_{ij}^{-}=S_{p}(f_{i},p_{j}^{-}),p_{j}^{+}\in P^{+},p_{j}^{-}\in P^{-}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ij}^{+}=S_{p}(f_{i},p_{j}^{+}),S_{ij}^{-}=S_{p}(f_{i},p_{j}^{-}),p_{j}^{+}\in P^{+},p_{j}^{-}\in P^{-},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{contra}}^{i}=\max\left(-\log\left(\frac{\sum\exp(S_{ij}^{+})}{\sum\exp(S_{ij}^{+})+\sum\exp(S_{ij}^{-})}\right),0\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>contra</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{contra}}^{i}=\max\left(-\log\left(\frac{\sum\exp(S_{ij}^{+})}{\sum\exp(S_{ij}^{+})+\sum\exp(S_{ij}^{-})}\right),0\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Prototype Center Loss enhances feature aggregation by minimizing the distance between sample features and class prototype centers, which are weighted representatives of the class. This encourages features to cluster around the prototype, controlled by the weight <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{center}}^{i}=\sigma/S_{p}(f_{i},\mu_{c})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>center</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{center}}^{i}=\sigma/S_{p}(f_{i},\mu_{c}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Negative Log-Likelihood Survival prediction Loss (NLLSurvLoss) adapts to the characteristics of censored data in survival analysis by utilizing the Negative Log-Likelihood Loss (NLL Loss) to quantify the discrepancy between the predicted risk and the actual survival status. Based on the predicted risk <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(s)</annotation></semantics></math> and survival function <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Surv(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Surv(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, it constructs loss terms for censored and uncensored samples, respectively, balancing the contribution weights of the two.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Surv(t)=\prod_{s=0}^{t-1}(1-h(s)),\quad h(s)\in[0,1]^{B\times K_{\text{time}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>time</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Surv(t)=\prod_{s=0}^{t-1}(1-h(s)),\quad h(s)\in[0,1]^{B\times K_{\text{time}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Uncensored sample loss quantifies the deviation between the predicted risk and the actual occurrence of events:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{UCs}=-(1-Cs)\cdot(\log(Surv(Y))+\log(h(Y)))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{UCs}=-(1-Cs)\cdot(\log(Surv(Y))+\log(h(Y))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Censored sample loss quantifies the deviation between the survival function and the censoring time:</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{Cs}=-Cs\cdot\log(Surv(Y+1))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{Cs}=-Cs\cdot\log(Surv(Y+1)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> represents the event time, while <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cs" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cs</annotation></semantics></math> is the censoring indicator (1 for censored, 0 for uncensored).</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{prototypes}}=L_{contra}+L_{center}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>prototypes</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{prototypes}}=L_{contra}+L_{center},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{surv}}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left[\left(1-\alpha_{loss}\right)\cdot\left(L_{UCs}^{\left(i\right)}+L_{Cs}^{\left(i\right)}\right)+\alpha_{loss}\cdot L_{Cs}^{\left(i\right)}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>surv</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{surv}}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left[\left(1-\alpha_{loss}\right)\cdot\left(L_{UCs}^{\left(i\right)}+L_{Cs}^{\left(i\right)}\right)+\alpha_{loss}\cdot L_{Cs}^{\left(i\right)}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{total}}=\beta_{loss}\cdot L_{\mathrm{prototypes}}+(1-\beta_{loss})\cdot L_{\mathrm{surv}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>total</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>prototypes</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>surv</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{total}}=\beta_{loss}\cdot L_{\mathrm{prototypes}}+(1-\beta_{loss})\cdot L_{\mathrm{surv}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/loss.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Schematic of ProtoSurv Loss: ProtoSurv Loss creates a prototype space focusing on intra-class convergence and inter-class separation, while NLLSurvLoss uses risk score stratification. Their combined approach enhances updates to the prototype library and survival analysis.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06113v1/kaplan_p.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/118;" width="476" height="118"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Kaplan-Meier survival curves and box plots of risk score distribution on the LUAD and BLCA datasets.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
