<tr id="S4.T1.3.27" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.27.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:13.0pt;padding-right:13.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.27.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CCL +FeatProto (Large)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.27.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:13.0pt;padding-right:13.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.27.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0.6947 ± 0.0302</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.27.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:13.0pt;padding-right:13.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.27.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.6918 ± 0.0281</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.27.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:13.0pt;padding-right:13.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.27.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.8774 ± 0.0152</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.27.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:13.0pt;padding-right:13.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.27.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.7215 ± 0.0401</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.27.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:13.0pt;padding-right:13.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.27.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">0.7464</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>C-index comparisons on four datasets. The best and second-best scores are in <span id="S4.T1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> and <span id="S4.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Underline</span>, respectively. Overall represents the average C-index across datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experiment Setups</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Dataset collection.</span><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> To demonstrate the performance of the proposed method, we utilized four publicly available cancer datasets from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA): Lung Adenocarcinoma (LUAD, n = 453) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, Bladder Urothelial Carcinoma (BLCA, n = 373) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, Glioblastoma &amp; Lower Grade Glioma (GBMLGG, n=568) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, and Uterine Corpus Endometrial Carcinoma (UCEC, n=480) </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Each dataset contains paired diagnostic WSI and genomic data with basic survival outcomes. Genomic profiles provide molecular information about individuals, including RNA sequencing (RNA-seq), copy number variation (CNV), single-nucleotide variation (SNV), and DNA methylation. To conduct a fairer comparison, we follow existing research works </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and use RNA-seq, CNV, and SNV data, which were further divided into six subcategories: 1) tumor suppression, 2) tumorigenesis, 3) protein kinase, 4) cell differentiation, 5) transcription, and 6) cytokine and growth.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Data preprocessing.</span><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Following the general processing practices in current research, the CLAM </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> model was employed for fully automated data preprocessing. For illustration, Fig. </span><a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-A Experiment Setups ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> depicts that for each WSI, automatic tissue segmentation was performed to exclude holes; subsequently, image patches capable of representing the entire region were extracted within these areas. As illustrated in Fig. </span><a href="#S4.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ IV-A Experiment Setups ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, the tissue regions were segmented into 256×256-sized patches at 20x magnification. For survival time binning, first divide the data into intervals based on the frequency of uncensored data, and then expand the leftmost and rightmost intervals to ensure that all data fall within these intervals. Fig. </span><a href="#S4.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IV-A Experiment Setups ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> presents the temporal binning results of the four datasets.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/wsi.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/324;" width="476" height="324" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>The processing procedure of WSIs. After removing the edges and hole regions, the WSIs are segmented into patches, which are then combined to form WSI bags.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/patch.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Architecture of the patch feature extraction model. A ResNet-50 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> network with residual connections processes input image patches, and the resulting patch-level features are generated for subsequent analysis tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/dataset.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/475;" width="476" height="475" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>The visual presentation of the binning results in all datasets shows the time ranges of four survival stages of different datasets, serving as the prediction labels.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Parameter Setting</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">FeatProto (Large)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">40 Typical, 5 Wandering Prototypes</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">FeatProto (Small)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">30 Typical, 5 Wandering Prototypes</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Parameter settings for FeatProto models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluation metrics.</span><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> The concordance index, known as the C-index, is a metric used to evaluate the performance of survival analysis models </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. It quantifies the model’s capability to correctly rank pairs of individuals according to their predicted survival outcomes. The C-index is formulated as follows:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{C-index}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i&lt;j}\mathcal{I}(T_{i}&lt;T_{j})\cdot(1-c_{j})\cdot\mathcal{I}(\hat{y}_{i}&gt;\hat{y}_{j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.900em">C-index</mtext><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{C-index}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i&lt;j}\mathcal{I}(T_{i}&lt;T_{j})\cdot(1-c_{j})\cdot\mathcal{I}(\hat{y}_{i}&gt;\hat{y}_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">where </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> denotes the total number of valid sample pairs (excluding pairs with </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}=T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}=T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> or invalid comparisons due to censoring); </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> represent the observed survival times of the </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">-th and </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">-th patients, respectively; </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> are the predicted risk scores for sample </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (with higher risk scores indicating shorter expected survival times); </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> is an indicator function that returns 1 if the argument holds true and 0 otherwise; and </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-c_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-c_{j})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> ensures that the </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">-th individual is uncensored, i.e., the survival time of </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> is observed rather than being truncated by right censorship.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Implementation details.</span><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Experiments were performed on a system featuring a 32 GB NVIDIA Tesla V100-SXM2 GPU alongside an AMD EPYC 7542 32-Core Processor. The software setup included Python 3.8.20 and PyTorch 2.4.1, utilizing the CUDA 12.1 acceleration library, and was deployed on Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS. To guarantee reproducibility, a random seed of 2,026 was consistently used throughout all procedural operations. The AdamW optimizer </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> was applied with a learning rate of 1e-3 and the cosine annealing learning rate scheduling algorithm across 30 epochs. Each cancer dataset was evaluated via 5-fold cross-validation with an 8:2 split. All methods used ResNet-50 </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> to extract features when utilizing the pathological modality, and shared identical hyperparameters.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06113v1/exp.png" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:952/352;" width="952" height="352" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 9: </span>Interpretability reasoning analysis of FeatProto: Input a pair of WSI and gene sequences, generate corresponding features. Through the MPMatch module, calculate the nearest similarity, average similarity, and center similarity. Then, conduct traceable analysis on the corresponding feature prototypes of the obtained results to obtain interpretable results.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparative Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">We evaluated our framework against various survival analysis models, including the genomics-based SNN </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, the pathology-based AttenMIL </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, and CLAM-SB </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, as well as other multimodal models. Below is an overview of selected multimodal competitors: MCAT </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> uses a co-attention mechanism to integrate genomic and pathological features through separate Transformers for survival prediction. CMTA </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> enhances modality-specific representations by integrating them with multimodal ones to convey complementary information. CCL </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> breaks down multimodal knowledge, employing cohort-guided modeling to improve generalization.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">As prototype learning methods, PIBD </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and MMP </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> both revolve around prototypes to support multimodal cancer survival prediction: the former models prototypes corresponding to different risk levels via its Prototypical Information Bottleneck module to select discriminative instances within each modality, and further decouples multimodal data under the guidance of a joint prototypical distribution using its Prototypical Information Disentanglement module; the latter unsupervisedly summarizes gigapixel pathological WSIs into morphological prototypes through Gaussian mixture models, encodes transcriptomic data into biological pathway prototypes, and ultimately fuses these condensed prototypes to fulfill the prediction task.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Unlike the two aforementioned prototype learning models, our method focuses on the interpretability of decision results and utilizes a feature prototype library to output matching schemes and corresponding sample sources during risk stratification.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hyperparameter setting</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">PMDSim provides the most effective characterization of feature prototype clustering, as demonstrated in Table </span><a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-C Hyperparameter setting ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. Compared to Euclidean distance, the power operation in PMDSim enhances the contrast between nearby features, thereby reducing the influence of outlier prototypes. Unlike Cosine distance, which prioritizes directional similarity, PMDSim emphasizes magnitude differences, making it better suited for medium-dimensional prototype spaces. Table </span><a href="#S4.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ IV-C Hyperparameter setting ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> presents the hyperparameter analysis of the power exponent </span><math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> in PMDSim, and results show that performance is optimal when </span><math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">In the multi-level similarity structure of MPMatch, optimal matching performance is achieved when higher weights are assigned to the </span><math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> levels. This is because the central prototype represented by the </span><math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> weight tends to overlook the finer distributional characteristics of the prototype library. Overreliance on </span><math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">sim</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\mathrm{sim}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> leads to mismatches, as reflected in the performance drops shown in Table </span><a href="#S4.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ IV-C Hyperparameter setting ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">. It can be seen that using the ratio </span><math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4:0.4:0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.4</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4:0.4:0.2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> achieves the best matching performance.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">As shown in Table </span><a href="#S4.T6" title="TABLE VI ‣ IV-C Hyperparameter setting ‣ IV experiment ‣ Multimodal Feature Prototype Learning for Interpretable and DiscriminativeCancer Survival Prediction" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, for the update parameter in EMA ProtoUp, a small </span><math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> leads to an overly high proportion of new prototypes, thus neglecting the position-preserving effect of old prototypes; conversely, a large </span><math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> results in too weak an influence of new prototypes, causing the prototype library to update slowly.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T3.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:9.0pt;padding-right:9.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Model</span></td>
